 ## Screen Reader Support
 
-SnowRaven uses semantic structure and ARIA attributes so assistive technology can describe the interface accurately. Where the navigation is a vertical list, its destinations and their panels are linked with the correct tab/tabpanel roles and selected state; where it is the phone bottom bar, each button marks itself as the current destination instead, and every panel carries its own name either way. Filter pills announce whether they are pressed, toggles announce on/off as switches, and sortable table columns announce their sort direction. A sort offered as a row of choices rather than a menu, such as Taxonomic and A–Z on the List Comparer or Newest and Oldest above a list of comments, marks the choice that is in force on the choice itself, so its highlight is never the only thing saying which sort you are looking at, and where those choices are drawn as one segmented control that control is a named group. The species selector exposes its expanded state and current option. The in-view marker lists use real list semantics, and collapsed filter panels are made inert so their hidden controls never become stray tab stops. Information that is shown only with an icon or a color (breeding-code tiers, map recency dots) carries a hidden text label so it is never lost to a screen reader. How a chart is described depends on how it is drawn, and the rule is the same everywhere it applies. A chart drawn as a GRAPHIC, where there is nothing in it for a screen reader to read, carries an image role and a concise text summary: that covers the line, bar, area and donut charts, among them life-list growth, checklists by month and weekday, temporal and media trends, and sightings over time. A chart drawn as ROWS OF TEXT does not, and that is deliberate rather than an omission: every value is already on its own row in words and numbers, so the bars beside them are marked decorative, and wrapping those rows in an image role would replace readable text with one long label. The Weather section's distribution and per-species charts on the Statistics tab are drawn that way, as are the Data Quality bars. Purely decorative chart flourishes are hidden from assistive tech either way. Regions that update on their own (the weather result, species and result counts, loading states, inline errors, and a keyboard tab move in the Tab Layout list) are announced as they change. When part of the app cannot load your saved eBird backup or Macaulay Library export, that failure is announced too: on all eight tabs that read your data (Statistics, the Calendar, Checklists, Breeding Codes, Named Birds, Species Detail, Multimedia and the Map Explorer) the message appears inside an alert region that is already present, and empty, from the moment the tab starts loading, rather than being created along with its text. A region that arrives with its first message is the standard way for an announcement to be missed, so the region is mounted first and filled afterwards, each message as a fresh child node. The region holds the sentence and nothing else: the alert icon beside it is hidden from assistive technology and the "Go to Settings" button sits outside it, so what is read out is the sentence itself. The Weather tab's checklist backlog reports a saved backup it cannot load in the same words and from the same kind of region. Because that one is a section you open and close rather than a tab, its region sits outside the part that collapses, so the region is in place before any message reaches it, rather than being created along with its text: opening the section fills a region that was already there, and closing the section empties that same region rather than removing it. The failure is announced when you open the section, and again each time you open it after that.
