         <li><strong>What is stored in iCloud.</strong> With iCloud Sync on, SnowRaven writes your eBird backup, your Macaulay Library export and each county's eBird bar-chart file into an iCloud container that belongs to your Apple ID, together with a small record for each file: its name, when it was uploaded, its size and a checksum of its contents, and which device it came from (the device's name, its kind, and a random identifier the app makes up on that device, which is not a hardware or account identifier). Your API keys are written only while the separate <strong>Sync API keys</strong> switch, described next, is also on. Your app settings, map preferences and cached lookups stay on each device and are never synced, with one exception: the day-by-day lists of species that eBird reported in a county, which the Targets tab fetches with your key, are written into the same container as one copy per device, each holding the day lists that device has, whether it fetched them from eBird or took them from your other devices' copies (the county, the day, when the list was asked for and whether the day was over by then, the list's size, and each species eBird listed with the time and place of that report as eBird gives them), along with the same small record each file has, so your other devices do not repeat those requests. They hold nothing from your own files.</li>
         <li><strong>Sync API keys.</strong> A second switch in the same section, off by default and available only while iCloud Sync is on, keeps your eBird and OpenWeather API keys the same across your own devices. With it on, SnowRaven writes one small record into the same private container holding, for each of the two keys, the key exactly as you entered it, when it was last changed, and which device changed it (the device's name, its kind, and the same random identifier). It goes only to your own iCloud account, and only your devices that also turn the switch on receive it. Apple encrypts the record in transit and at rest; it is end-to-end encrypted only if Advanced Data Protection is turned on for your iCloud account, and without it Apple's standard iCloud protection applies, the same as for your synced files. The developer has no way to see it. Turning the switch off leaves the keys on each device in place and removes the copy from iCloud, and <strong>Remove synced keys from iCloud</strong> deletes the copy at any time without touching any device.</li>
         <li><strong>Whose account.</strong> The copies live only in your own iCloud account, on Apple's servers, and move only between devices signed in to that account. There is no SnowRaven server in the path, and the developer has no way to see, receive, or access what you sync. The container is private to the app and does not appear in iCloud Drive's file listing. Apple's handling of iCloud data is described in <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apple's privacy policy</a>.</li>
+        <li><strong>Copy iCloud details.</strong> If sync is not working, <strong>Copy iCloud details</strong> at the foot of the iCloud Sync section in Settings puts a plain-text report on your clipboard, for you to paste into a message to the developer if you choose. It lists what iCloud reports for each synced file: the app's version, your devices' names and the random identifier the app made up for each, whether iCloud is available, county codes, file sizes and dates, sync states, and Apple's error codes and messages. It holds no file contents, no file names you chose and no API keys. SnowRaven never sends it anywhere itself: it reaches the developer only if you paste it into a message.</li>
         <li><strong>Turning it off and removing the copies.</strong> Switch iCloud Sync off at any time in Settings; the files on each device stay in place and keep working, and Sync API keys goes off with it. <strong>Remove synced files from iCloud</strong> in the same section deletes the file copies in your iCloud account, the bar-chart files and the day-by-day lists included, without touching any device, and <strong>Remove synced keys from iCloud</strong> does the same for the key copy. Deleting the app or its data on one device does not by itself remove the copies in iCloud, so use those controls first if you want iCloud empty.</li>
       </ul>
 
